package pages

templ InternalsLiteralsPage() {
	<h1>Literals</h1>
	<p>Ghoti has numeric and identifier literals that carry intrinsic type information with their usage.</p>
	<h2>Identifiers</h2>
	<h3>Booleans</h3>
	<ul>
		<li><code>true</code></li>
		<li><code>false</code></li>
	</ul>
	<h3>Null Pointer</h3>
	<ul>
		<li><code>nullptr</code></li>
	</ul>
	<p>This literal represents a null pointer value. It has a special primitive null pointer type that implicitly coerces to any constant or mutable pointer type (<code>^T</code> or <code>^mut T</code>).</p>
	<h3>Undefined</h3>
	<ul>
		<li><code>undefined</code></li>
	</ul>
	<p>This literal is used to explicitly denote that the assignee's value is undefined. Reading from undefined memory is illegal and safety checked when possible.</p>
	<p>This can be assigned to any type, including as a call argument or member initializer. There are no restrictions on the usage of this literal for initialization.</p>
	<h2>Numeric Literals</h2>
	<p>All numbers in ghoti can be broken into sections with an underscore. The underscore may be placed in between any two digits, meaning it can neither lead or end any part of a literal (exponent included). For example:</p>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `0b1100_0011;   // Legal
0b1100_0011_;  // Illegal
3.14_15_92;    // Legal
3_.14_15_92;   // Illegal
3.14_15_92_;   // Illegal
3e14;          // Legal
3_e14;         // Illegal` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<p>In all of these cases, the underscore has no impact on the resulting value of the numeric literal. Internally, the underscores are stripped out before constexpr evaluation and code generation.</p>
	<p>It is recommended to use underscores in numbers when there is a logical grouping of digits for your use case. They are implemented in such a way that there is a high amount of freedom for the programmer to convey the most meaning with their code.</p>
	<h3>Trailing Numeric Literals</h3>
	<p>To pin the exact type of a numeric literal, you may use the following suffixes. They are also discussed in the <a href="/doc/syntax/types">type</a> documentation.</p>
	<p>An <em>unsuffixed</em> integer literal has type <a href="/doc/syntax/types"><code>constexpr_int</code></a>: it is compile-time known, coerces to any concrete numeric type it fits (integer <em>or</em> float), and folds at 128-bit precision. An unsuffixed real literal is likewise <code>constexpr_float</code> and coerces to any concrete float. A suffix removes that flexibility and fixes the type outright.</p>
	<ul>
		<li>A <code>constexpr_int</code> whose folded value does not fit its concrete integer target is a compile error (no silent wrap); a leading <code>-</code> can still bring <code>-(2<sup>N-1</sup>)</code> into range.</li>
		<li>An un-anchored <code>constexpr_int</code> / <code>constexpr_float</code> materializes as <code>i32</code> / <code>f64</code> at a <code>var</code> binding, a generic / <code>auto</code> parameter, an inferred return type, a struct field, or any runtime use. An un-annotated <code>const</code> keeps the constexpr type, so <code>const k := 5;</code> can still coerce to <code>u64</code> later.</li>
	</ul>
	<h4>Integer Suffixes</h4>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Suffix</th>
				<th>Type</th>
				<th>Example</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>None</td>
				<td><code>constexpr_int</code> (materializes as <code>i32</code>)</td>
				<td><code>42</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>i&lt;W&gt;</code></td>
				<td><code>iW</code>: a signed integer exactly <code>W</code> bits wide (1-65535)</td>
				<td><code>1i9</code>, <code>-3i128</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>u&lt;W&gt;</code></td>
				<td><code>uW</code>: an unsigned integer exactly <code>W</code> bits wide (1-65535)</td>
				<td><code>7u8</code>, <code>255u65535</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>z</code></td>
				<td><code>isize</code></td>
				<td><code>7z</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>uz</code></td>
				<td><code>usize</code></td>
				<td><code>7uz</code></td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>The old <code>l</code> / <code>L</code> (long), bare <code>u</code>, bare <code>i</code>, and <code>ul</code> suffixes have been removed. Write the width you mean: <code>1i64</code> instead of <code>1l</code>, <code>1u32</code> instead of <code>1u</code>.</p>
	<h4>Float Suffixes</h4>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Suffix</th>
				<th>Type</th>
				<th>Example</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>None</td>
				<td><code>constexpr_float</code> (materializes as <code>f64</code>)</td>
				<td><code>3.14</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>f16</code></td>
				<td><code>f16</code> (IEEE-754 half)</td>
				<td><code>1.5f16</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>f32</code></td>
				<td><code>f32</code> (IEEE-754 single)</td>
				<td><code>1.5f32</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>f64</code></td>
				<td><code>f64</code> (IEEE-754 double)</td>
				<td><code>1.5f64</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>f80</code></td>
				<td><code>f80</code> (x86 80-bit extended; x86 targets only)</td>
				<td><code>1.5f80</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>f128</code></td>
				<td><code>f128</code> (IEEE-754 quad)</td>
				<td><code>1.5f128</code></td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>The old bare <code>f</code> / <code>F</code> suffix has been removed; use <code>f32</code> explicitly.</p>
	<h3>Integer Bases</h3>
	<p>Integer literals may be prefixed to denote a base:</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Prefix</th>
				<th>Base</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>None</td>
				<td>10</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>0o</code></td>
				<td>8</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>0x</code></td>
				<td>16</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>0b</code></td>
				<td>2</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>Conformance to the bases digit set is enforced during tokenization.</p>
	<h2>Text Literals</h2>
	<h3>Escape Sequences</h3>
	<p>String literals, character literals, and raw identifiers understand the same escapes. Multiline <code>{ `\\` }</code> strings take their text verbatim.</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Escape</th>
				<th>Meaning</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><code>{ `\n \r \t \0` }</code></td>
				<td>newline, carriage return, tab, and the zero byte</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>{ `\\ \' \"` }</code></td>
				<td>a backslash or a quote</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>{ `\xHH` }</code></td>
				<td>one byte, given as exactly two hex digits. In a string it may make the bytes invalid UTF-8, which is allowed: a string is bytes.</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>{ `\u{H...}` }</code></td>
				<td>a Unicode scalar value, given as one to six hex digits, encoded as UTF-8. Surrogates and values past <code>U+10FFFF</code> are rejected.</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>A string's <code>.len</code> counts its encoded bytes: <code>{ `"\u{1F600}".len` }</code> is <code>4</code>. A malformed escape is reported at the escape itself.</p>
	<h3>Character Literals</h3>
	<p>A character literal is the code point of exactly one character or escape: <code>{ `'a'` }</code>, <code>{ `'é'` }</code>, <code>{ `'😀'` }</code>, <code>{ `'\u{1F600}'` }</code>, <code>{ `'\n'` }</code>. It is an untyped integer constant, so it takes whatever integer type holds it (<code>{ `const b: u8 = 'a';` }</code>, <code>{ `s[i] == 'x'` }</code>), and on its own it is a <code>u21</code>, the smallest type that holds every code point: <code>{ `var c := 'a';` }</code> is a <code>u21</code>. <code>{ `'\xFF'` }</code> is the value 255. A literal with more than one code point, like <code>{ `'ab'` }</code> or a family emoji joined from five, is an error.</p>
	<h3>Raw Identifiers</h3>
	<p><code>{ `@"..."` }</code> names anything, including keywords and any Unicode text: <code>{ `const @"😀" := 1;` }</code>. Escapes decode first, so <code>{ `@"\u{1F600}"` }</code> is the same name. Names compare byte for byte, with no Unicode normalization: a precomposed <code>é</code> and an <code>e</code> followed by a combining accent are different names. A raw identifier must be valid UTF-8 and not empty.</p>
}
